 1043: #  ---------------------------------------------------------------- #497 widthkillclass
 1044: #  *** THIS PROBE IS FOR AN SGI/MIPS MACHINE AND IT IS WIRED INTO THE GATE NAMED `arm`.
 1045: #  That is following a precedent, not endorsing it. ***  This gate already runs
 1046: #  footbridge_sites_probe.py and pit8253_latch_probe.py -- neither of which is an ARM
 1047: #  instruction test either -- so it has become the tree's general DEVICE-probe gate by
 1048: #  accretion.  *** BUT THOSE TWO AT LEAST RUN THIS GATE'S OWN `-E cats` MACHINE, AND
 1049: #  THIS ONE DOES NOT: it is the FIRST probe here on a foreign machine AND CPU family.
 1050: #  The PIT wiring comment says outright that it was placed here BECAUSE this is the only
 1051: #  gate whose machine it shares. ***  So this is not a third instance of an existing
 1052: #  accretion; it is a new kind of one, and the sentence above is true while its
 1053: #  implication was generous to the decision it defends.  The architecture-named alternative, gate_mips.sh, is an 82-line RIG-BOOT
 1054: #  gate with no probe infrastructure at all; putting a 35-row detector there would change
 1055: #  what that gate is for.
 1056: #
 1057: #  Recorded rather than silently compounded: the right answer is a gate_devices.sh split,
 1058: #  which is a harness round of its own and owes its own controls.  FILED as
 1059: #  `gatedevicesplit`, with a forcing criterion: the NEXT device probe wired into
 1060: #  this gate converts it from filed to OWED.
 1061: #  *** #498 sgidewidth.  WIRING THIS PROBE FIRES A PRE-SPECIFIED TRIGGER, AND THE TRIGGER
 1062: #  IS HONOURED RATHER THAN QUIETLY PASSED. ***  The #497 block below filed `gatedevicesplit`
 1063: #  with a forcing criterion in its own words: "the NEXT device probe wired into this gate
 1064: #  converts it from filed to OWED."  This is that probe.  The row is moved to OWED in the
 1065: #  same commit -- a criterion that fires and is ignored is worse than one never written,
 1066: #  because the next reader trusts it.
 1067: #
 1068: #  It is wired HERE anyway, and deliberately: the split is a harness round owing its own
 1069: #  controls, and holding a HIGH device fix hostage to a gate refactor would be the tail
 1070: #  wagging the dog.  What is NOT acceptable is compounding it silently.
 1071: DEWLOG=$LOGDIR/sgi_de_width.log
 1072: python3 sgi_de_width_probe.py "$PMAX" gate > "$DEWLOG" 2>&1 || true
 1073: 
 1074: #  SENTINEL FIRST -- a preserved log is verified by its completion sentinel, never by its
 1075: #  existence.  A killed run loses every row and looks exactly like one that produced none.
 1076: if ! grep -q "OPUS498T_DONE" "$DEWLOG"; then
 1077:     note "sgi_de width probe did not reach its completion sentinel; last lines follow"
 1078:     tail -5 "$DEWLOG" | sed 's/^/       /'
 1079:     check "sgi_de width probe completed" "no" "yes"
 1080: else
 1081:     check "sgi_de width probe completed" "yes" "yes"
 1082: 
 1083:     #  CONTROLS BY NAME.  A detector whose liveness and device-signature rows are dead
 1084:     #  scores well on a corpse: every access row would read "no message" and pass.
 1085:     check "sgi_de controls live+devsig" \
 1086:           "$(grep -q 'OPUS498T_CONTROL=OK' "$DEWLOG" && echo yes || echo no)" "yes"
 1087: 
 1088:     #  PER-ROW PASS, one line, checked individually.  A row that silently stops being
 1089:     #  tested is the padded-column class -- a name as long as its column makes the check
 1090:     #  unsatisfiable and the row quietly stops being tested.
 1091:     #  *** `tail_acc_x' PINS A HOST DEATH ON PURPOSE AND WILL GO RED BY SUCCEEDING. ***
 1092:     #  It observes `sgideprimop' -- an accepted-width access with startFlag reaches
 1093:     #  draw_primitive(), whose default arm is fatal on 254 of 256 top-byte op classes,
 1094:     #  two of which (POINT 0x00, TRIANGLE 0x02) are DOCUMENTED primitives.
 1095:     #
 1096:     #  *** THE BLAST RADIUS HAS THREE TIERS, NOT ONE, AND THIS NOTE SAID `BOTH ROWS' UNTIL
 1097:     #  2026-08-31.  Corrected after the grok seat read the oracles against the code paths
 1098:     #  they exercise and found the overstatement. ***
 1099:     #
 1100:     #    tail_acc_x            CERTAIN red -- wants exit1, and the fix removes the death.
 1101:     #    tail_acc              CONDITIONAL -- it counts the DRAW message SUBSTRING and
 1102:     #                          wants 1.  A fix that LATCHES the text without REWORDING it
 1103:     #                          still prints the first occurrence, so the row stays GREEN.
 1104:     #                          It reds only if the message is reworded.
 1105:     #    hi_draw, hi_rd_draw   NEITHER red NOR safe.  Both want count(DRAW) == 0, so a
 1106:     #                          REWORD leaves them green because the string no longer
 1107:     #                          exists -- GREEN FOR THE WRONG REASON, i.e. VACUOUS BY
 1108:     #                          CONSTRUCTION.  A reword silently blinds them; it does not
 1109:     #                          fail them, and nothing would say so.
 1110:     #
 1111:     #  *** SO THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT IS: KEEP THE MESSAGE TEXT AND ADD `(once)'. ***  That
 1112:     #  costs ONE oracle re-pin (tail_acc_x).  Rewording costs two re-pins AND silently
 1113:     #  blinds two further rows -- which is the worse half and is invisible to any gate.
 1114:     #  *** AND THE OBVIOUS RE-PIN IS A TRAP.  DO NOT RE-PIN `tail_acc_x' ONTO `"exit0"'. ***
 1115:     #  Adjudicated 2026-08-31 by the flagship seat, which traced the channel rather than the
 1116:     #  intent.  The row wants `exit1' today; the natural correction is `exit0' ("the host now
 1117:     #  survives").  Trace what that oracle can see:
 1118:     #
 1119:     #    a future one-token death reinstatement -- `exit(0)' at the default arm instead of
 1120:     #    `exit(1)' -- prints the announce once and then DIES.  `how' is then "exit0", so a
 1121:     #    re-pinned tail_acc_x reads GREEN; `tail_acc' still counts its one message, GREEN;
 1122:     #    and no other session reaches draw_primitive() at all.  TOTAL ESCAPE, 29/29.
 1123:     #
 1124:     #  So the re-pin would MINT, at the exact site being fixed, the exact blindness the queue
 1125:     #  row `exit0oracle' was filed to describe: EXIT STATUS CANNOT VARY between `declined and
 1126:     #  then quit' and `died with status 0'.  That is knowable at WRITING time from the CHANNEL
 1127:     #  check -- name the mutant a row kills and the observable that differs under it -- and it
 1128:     #  is why that check is run before the table, not after.
 1129:     #
 1130:     #  RE-PIN ONTO A POSITIVE POST-DRAW OBSERVABLE INSTEAD: extend the session with a load
 1131:     #  through the device and assert the VALUE (`printed()' already exists in the probe and
 1132:     #  reads "died" when the host is gone).  An exit-status row may stay as a SECOND channel;
 1133:     #  the value row is the load-bearing one.  This is L1 -- a row under construction -- so
 1134:     #  there is no stopping-rule tension in fixing it in-round.
 1135:     #
 1136:     #  The fixing round therefore owes a FOURTH mutant beside the three already enumerated:
 1137:     #  death-by-`exit(0)' at the default arm, which the re-pinned row must kill.
 1138:     #
 1139:     #  Oracle-correction ordering applies -- re-pin FIRST, measure the committed build red,
 1140:     #  THEN edit.  Without this note the red reads as a regression in THIS device.
 1141:     for v in "live" "devsig" "lo_kill" "lo_decl" "lo_alive" "lo_sent" "lo_latch" "lo_kill2" "lo_decl2" "lo_alive2" "lo_sent2" "hi_draw" "hi_alive" "hi_rd_draw" "hi_rd_alive" "hi_rd_sent" "two_widths_n" "two_widths_1" "two_widths_2" "dec_switch" "hi_kill" "tail_acc" "tail_acc_x" "tail_acc_h" "acc4_last" "acc8_hi32" "acc8_lo32" "acc8_read" "re_first" "sharedlatch"; do
 1142:         check "sgi_de row: $v" "$(count "$DEWLOG" "^$v  .*ok$")" "1"
 1143:     done
 1144: 
 1145:     #  DENOMINATOR, SUMMED ARITHMETICALLY.  Two rows, not one: a single "22 ok" check
 1146:     #  cannot tell a LOST row from a FAILING one, and those need different responses.
 1147:     #  The sum is arithmetic rather than a string join -- concatenating two counts and
 1148:     #  comparing to "220" is satisfied by (22 ok, 0 FAIL) AND by (2 ok, 20 FAIL), which
 1149:     #  is a defect this gate has already shipped once.
 1150:     check "sgi_de rows emitted" \
 1151:           "$(( $(count "$DEWLOG" " ok$") + $(count "$DEWLOG" " FAIL$") ))" "30"
 1152:     check "sgi_de rows green" "$(count "$DEWLOG" " ok$")" "30"
 1153: fi
 1154: 
 1155: SGILOG=$LOGDIR/sgi_re_width.log
 1156: python3 sgi_re_width_probe.py "$PMAX" gate > "$SGILOG" 2>&1 || true
 1157: 
